{"seq":1,"slug":"measuring-information","title":"度量信息","docCount":2,"url":"/l-doc-books/c/people/claude-shannon/ideas-and-methods/measuring-information","docs":[{"seq":1,"slug":"information-as-uncertainty","title":"信息即不确定性","url":"/l-doc-books/c/people/claude-shannon/ideas-and-methods/measuring-information/information-as-uncertainty","wordCount":1810,"readingTime":8},{"seq":2,"slug":"the-bit-as-unit","title":"比特：信息的计量单位","url":"/l-doc-books/c/people/claude-shannon/ideas-and-methods/measuring-information/the-bit-as-unit","wordCount":1748,"readingTime":7}],"introHtml":"<h1 id=\"度量信息\">度量信息</h1><p>在香农之前，&quot;信息&quot;是个谁都会用却谁也说不清的词。工程师知道电报比电话省线路，知道有些消息&quot;含金量&quot;更高，但没人能说出一条消息究竟&quot;有多少&quot;信息——就像在温度计发明之前，人们能感觉冷热却无法说&quot;今天 23 度&quot;。香农做的，正是为信息造出那支温度计。</p>\n<p>他的突破不在于发明了新公式，而在于换了一个问题。别人问&quot;这条消息说了什么&quot;，他问&quot;在收到它之前，接收方有多不确定&quot;。一旦把信息和不确定性挂钩，度量就有了着落：消息消除的不确定性越多，它携带的信息就越多。一条&quot;太阳从东边升起&quot;的消息几乎不含信息，因为你早就确定；而一条&quot;今天某地发生地震&quot;的消息信息量极大，因为它出人意料。</p>\n<p>这一章讲两件事。第一篇拆解&quot;信息即不确定性&quot;这个反直觉的核心洞见，看香农如何用概率把&quot;惊讶程度&quot;变成可以相加的量，并最终凝结成那个著名的熵公式 <span class=\"math-inline\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=-\\sum_i p_i\\log_2 p_i</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0813em;\">H</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0497em;vertical-align:-0.2997em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\"><span class=\"mop op-symbol small-op\" style=\"position:relative;top:0em;\">∑</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.162em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.4003em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2997em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\"><span class=\"mop\">lo<span style=\"margin-right:0.0139em;\">g</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.207em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.4559em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2441em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>。第二篇讲他选择的单位——比特，看他为什么用以 2 为底的对数，为什么&quot;一次二选一&quot;恰好是信息的最小原子，以及这个单位如何从抽象定义变成了今天我们存储和传输一切的通用货币。</p>\n<p>读这一章不需要高深数学，只要愿意接受一个略反常识的视角：信息的多少，不取决于内容本身，而取决于它从多少种可能里把现实&quot;挑&quot;了出来。理解了这一点，后面信道、编码、压缩的一切，都顺理成章。</p>\n"}